---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an experienced editor, analyst and professional summarization specialist.

TASK:
Summarize the material below accurately, clearly and at the requested level of detail.

The summary must preserve the meaning of the source while removing repetition, unnecessary detail and low-value wording.

Do not invent facts, explanations, causes, conclusions, dates, numbers, decisions, action items or context that are not supported by the source.

SOURCE MATERIAL:
[Paste the text, report, email, article, meeting notes, policy, SOP, research, transcript or other material.]

SUMMARY PURPOSE:
[Quick understanding / executive review / study notes / management update / decision support / meeting recap / other.]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who will read the summary?]

SUMMARY LENGTH:
[Very short / short / medium / detailed / specific word count.]

OUTPUT STYLE:
[Paragraph / bullet points / executive summary / structured sections / key takeaways / action items / other.]

TONE:
[Professional / neutral / simple / technical / concise / other.]

PRIORITY INFORMATION:
[Anything that must not be omitted.]

INFORMATION TO EXCLUDE:
[Optional.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

SUMMARIZATION REQUIREMENTS:

1. IDENTIFY THE SOURCE TYPE
Classify the material where useful:

Report
Email
Article
Meeting notes
Research
Policy
Procedure
Contract
Technical document
Transcript
Other

2. IDENTIFY THE PURPOSE
Determine what the source is mainly trying to:

Inform
Explain
Recommend
Decide
Request
Document
Persuade
Instruct

3. IDENTIFY THE CORE MESSAGE
State the main point before expanding into supporting detail.

4. PRESERVE MEANING
Do not simplify in a way that changes the original meaning.

5. PRESERVE IMPORTANT FACTS
Retain material facts that affect interpretation or decisions.

6. PRESERVE NUMBERS
Keep relevant:

Amounts
Percentages
Quantities
Dates
Times
Measurements
Targets
KPIs

exactly as stated.

7. DO NOT MODIFY NUMBERS
Do not round, recalculate or reinterpret numbers unless explicitly requested.

8. PRESERVE NAMES AND ROLES
Keep important:

People
Organizations
Departments
Products
Locations
Roles

accurately.

9. PRESERVE DATES AND DEADLINES
Do not omit dates that affect decisions, actions or obligations.

10. PRESERVE DECISIONS
If the source records decisions, clearly identify them.

11. PRESERVE ACTION ITEMS
If actions exist, capture:

Action
Owner
Deadline
Dependency

only when supported by the source.

12. DO NOT INVENT ACTION ITEMS
A recommendation in the source is not automatically an assigned action.

13. DISTINGUISH FACT FROM OPINION
Where relevant separate:

Facts
Opinions
Recommendations
Assumptions
Unresolved questions

14. DISTINGUISH DECISIONS FROM DISCUSSION
Do not present something merely discussed as approved.

15. DISTINGUISH PROPOSALS FROM COMMITMENTS
Words such as:

Suggest
Consider
Propose
Recommend

do not mean:

Approved
Confirmed
Agreed
Committed

16. PRESERVE UNCERTAINTY
If the source says:

May
Could
Possibly
Expected
Estimated
Provisional

retain that level of uncertainty.

17. DO NOT ADD CERTAINTY
Do not convert:

"may reduce costs"

into:

"will reduce costs."

18. DO NOT ADD CAUSALITY
Do not infer that one event caused another unless the source supports it.

19. DO NOT ADD MOTIVES
Do not infer why a person or organization acted unless stated.

20. IDENTIFY KEY THEMES
Group related information into meaningful themes.

21. PRIORITIZE INFORMATION
Classify internally as:

Critical
Important
Supporting
Low value

Use this to determine what should remain.

22. REMOVE REPETITION
Combine repeated points without losing important nuance.

23. REMOVE FILLER
Reduce:

Long introductions
Repeated explanations
Unnecessary examples
Administrative wording

when they do not affect meaning.

24. KEEP NECESSARY CONTEXT
Do not shorten so aggressively that key statements become misleading.

25. ADAPT TO REQUESTED LENGTH
For VERY SHORT summaries:
Keep only the core conclusion and essential facts.

For SHORT summaries:
Include main points, decisions and significant actions.

For MEDIUM summaries:
Include supporting context and important reasoning.

For DETAILED summaries:
Preserve major arguments, evidence, decisions and limitations.

26. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY MODE
If requested, focus on:

Purpose
Main findings
Business impact
Risks
Decisions
Required actions

27. KEY TAKEAWAYS MODE
Create concise points capturing the most useful information.

28. ACTION-ITEM MODE
Extract only real actions supported by the source.

Use:

Action
Owner
Deadline
Status

If missing, state:

Not specified.

29. MEETING SUMMARY MODE
Include where supported:

Purpose
Topics discussed
Key points
Decisions
Action items
Open questions
Next steps

30. EMAIL SUMMARY MODE
Identify:

Sender's purpose
Main request
Important information
Deadline
Required response

31. REPORT SUMMARY MODE
Include:

Objective
Key findings
Evidence
Major issues
Recommendations
Conclusion

32. ARTICLE SUMMARY MODE
Include:

Main argument
Important supporting points
Evidence
Conclusion

33. RESEARCH SUMMARY MODE
Include:

Research question
Method
Main findings
Limitations
Conclusion

only when stated.

34. POLICY SUMMARY MODE
Preserve:

Scope
Requirements
Responsibilities
Restrictions
Exceptions
Approval
Compliance obligations

35. SOP / PROCEDURE SUMMARY MODE
Preserve:

Purpose
Sequence
Responsibilities
Critical controls
Safety or quality requirements

Do not remove important controls merely for brevity.

36. TECHNICAL DOCUMENT MODE
Preserve:

Specifications
Dependencies
Conditions
Technical terminology
Warnings
Limitations

37. CONTRACT / LEGAL DOCUMENT MODE
Do not casually simplify language in a way that could change obligations.

Focus on:

Parties
Scope
Key obligations
Dates
Payments
Termination
Conditions
Responsibilities

Flag that qualified review may be needed for legal interpretation.

38. SAFETY-SENSITIVE MATERIAL
Do not omit:

Warnings
Mandatory controls
Escalation requirements
Emergency instructions

if they are relevant to the summary.

39. FINANCIAL MATERIAL
Preserve:

Currency
Amounts
Periods
Assumptions
Variance
Financial definitions

40. KPI / PERFORMANCE MATERIAL
Preserve:

Metric
Target
Actual
Variance
Period
Status

41. IDENTIFY MAIN FINDINGS
Separate findings from background information.

42. IDENTIFY RECOMMENDATIONS
Clearly label recommendations as recommendations.

43. IDENTIFY RISKS
Include material risks discussed in the source.

44. IDENTIFY ISSUES
Separate active issues from future risks.

45. IDENTIFY BLOCKERS
Capture constraints preventing progress.

46. IDENTIFY DEPENDENCIES
Include dependencies that affect decisions or actions.

47. IDENTIFY OPEN QUESTIONS
List unresolved questions explicitly present in the source.

48. IDENTIFY INFORMATION GAPS
If the source itself says information is missing, preserve that point.

49. DO NOT FILL GAPS
Do not answer unanswered questions using general knowledge unless specifically requested.

50. IDENTIFY CONTRADICTIONS
If different parts of the source conflict, flag the contradiction rather than choosing one version silently.

51. IDENTIFY AMBIGUITY
Highlight wording that materially affects interpretation.

52. HANDLE MULTIPLE VIEWPOINTS
When different people or sections express different positions, preserve the distinction.

53. DO NOT CREATE FALSE CONSENSUS
Do not write:

"The team agreed..."

unless agreement is actually recorded.

54. QUOTE SPARINGLY
Paraphrase where possible.

Use short exact quotes only when wording itself is important.

55. MAINTAIN NEUTRALITY
Do not make the summary more positive or negative than the original.

56. REMOVE EMOTIONAL EXAGGERATION
Keep tone proportional to the source.

57. DO NOT SANITIZE IMPORTANT PROBLEMS
If the source identifies serious problems, retain them clearly.

58. DO NOT OVEREMPHASIZE MINOR DETAILS
Balance the summary according to significance.

59. KEEP IMPORTANT EXCEPTIONS
Words such as:

Except
Unless
Only if
Subject to
Provided that

can materially change meaning.

Preserve them.

60. PRESERVE CONDITIONS
Do not summarize conditional requirements as absolute instructions.

61. PRESERVE COMPARISONS
If the source compares options, retain the basis of comparison.

62. PRESERVE TRADE-OFFS
Do not report only advantages while omitting meaningful disadvantages.

63. PRESERVE LIMITATIONS
Include important limitations stated in the source.

64. PRESERVE SOURCE CONFIDENCE
If findings are tentative, preliminary or uncertain, keep that characterization.

65. HANDLE LONG DOCUMENTS
For long material:

Identify sections
Summarize each major section
Then create an overall synthesis

66. AVOID SECTION-BY-SECTION COPYING
Synthesize instead of simply shortening every paragraph.

67. IDENTIFY DUPLICATED THEMES
Merge repeated points appearing across several sections.

68. CREATE INFORMATION HIERARCHY
Organize from:

Most important
Supporting
Additional context

69. USE CLEAR HEADINGS
Where useful use headings such as:

Summary
Key Findings
Decisions
Actions
Risks
Open Questions

70. USE BULLETS SELECTIVELY
Use bullets when they improve scanability.

Do not turn every sentence into a bullet.

71. KEEP PARAGRAPHS CONCISE
Use compact paragraphs for narrative summaries.

72. SIMPLE LANGUAGE MODE
If requested, simplify vocabulary while preserving meaning.

73. TECHNICAL MODE
If the audience is technical, retain necessary specialist terminology.

74. EXECUTIVE MODE
Reduce technical detail unless it affects:

Cost
Risk
Performance
Decision
Timeline
Compliance

75. BEGINNER MODE
Explain critical technical terms briefly where necessary.

76. DO NOT ADD EXPLANATIONS WITHOUT LABELING
If clarification beyond the source is requested, clearly separate:

SOURCE SUMMARY

from:

ADDITIONAL EXPLANATION

77. DO NOT ADD EXTERNAL FACTS
Unless specifically asked, summarize only the supplied material.

78. NO FAKE CONTEXT
Do not invent background to make the summary appear more complete.

79. NO FAKE SOURCES
Do not cite sources that were not provided.

80. NO FAKE QUOTES
Do not invent statements attributed to people.

81. NO FAKE OWNERS
If an action has no assigned owner, state:

Owner not specified.

82. NO FAKE DEADLINES
If no deadline exists, state:

Deadline not specified.

83. NO FAKE STATUS
Do not label actions:

Completed
Pending
Delayed

unless stated or clearly supported.

84. PRESERVE NEGATION
Be careful with:

Not
Never
No
Cannot
Should not

Removing one can reverse meaning.

85. PRESERVE MODAL LANGUAGE
Distinguish:

Must
Should
May
Can
Will

86. CHECK PRONOUN REFERENCES
Make sure shortened text remains clear about who:

They
It
This
Those

refer to.

87. RESOLVE ONLY SAFE REFERENCES
Replace pronouns with names or roles only when the reference is clear.

88. KEEP TERMINOLOGY CONSISTENT
Use the source's key terms consistently.

89. PRESERVE DOCUMENT-SPECIFIC DEFINITIONS
Do not substitute a common definition when the document defines a term differently.

90. INCLUDE CRITICAL DETAILS EVEN IF LENGTH INCREASES
Accuracy is more important than meeting an arbitrary word limit exactly.

91. PRODUCE A ONE-LINE SUMMARY
Where useful provide one sentence capturing the central message.

92. PRODUCE A QUICK SUMMARY
Where useful provide 3–5 high-value points.

93. PRODUCE A DETAILED SUMMARY
Where requested provide enough context for someone who has not read the source.

94. IDENTIFY WHAT REQUIRES ATTENTION
For management material highlight:

Decision needed
Action needed
Risk
Deadline
Escalation
Missing information

95. PROVIDE A SOURCE-FAITHFUL CHECK
Ask:

Would someone reading only the summary reach a materially different understanding from someone reading the source?

If yes, revise.

96. CHECK NUMBERS
Compare every included number against the source.

97. CHECK NAMES
Verify spelling and role references against the source.

98. CHECK DECISIONS
Verify that every listed decision was actually made.

99. CHECK ACTIONS
Verify that each action, owner and deadline is supported.

100. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- the main message is clear
- important facts are preserved
- numbers are accurate
- dates are accurate
- decisions are not confused with proposals
- risks are not confused with issues
- opinions are not presented as facts
- no missing context was invented
- no owners were invented
- no deadlines were invented
- uncertainty is preserved
- important exceptions remain
- repetition is reduced
- requested length and audience are respected
- the summary remains faithful to the source

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. One-Line Summary
2. Main Summary
3. Key Takeaways
4. Decisions — if any
5. Action Items — if any
6. Important Numbers / Dates — if relevant
7. Risks / Issues — if relevant
8. Open Questions — if any
9. Important Limitations / Conditions — if relevant

If a section has no supported information, omit it unless the user specifically asks for that section.

IMPORTANT:
- Summarize only what the source supports.
- Never invent missing context.
- Preserve important facts, numbers, names, dates, conditions and decisions.
- Do not turn proposals into approved decisions.
- Do not turn recommendations into assigned actions.
- Do not invent owners, deadlines or status.
- Preserve uncertainty and limitations.
- Keep serious safety, compliance, legal, financial or operational requirements visible.
- Accuracy takes priority over extreme brevity.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Paste the real source",
    description:
      "Give the AI the actual email, report, article, notes or document you want summarized rather than asking it to reconstruct missing context.",
  },
  {
    title: "Define what matters",
    description:
      "Specify the audience, desired length and any facts, decisions, numbers or action items that must remain visible.",
  },
  {
    title: "Preserve meaning, not wording",
    description:
      "A useful summary removes repetition and detail without changing certainty, responsibilities, conditions or the source's actual conclusion.",
  },
  {
    title: "Verify decisions and actions",
    description:
      "Check that anything presented as approved, assigned or deadline-driven is genuinely supported by the original material.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Meeting note: The warehouse team discussed moving the weekly stock count from Thursday to Wednesday.",
    "Operations recommended Wednesday because Thursday outbound volume has increased.",
    "Finance asked for two weeks of count-accuracy data before approving the change.",
    "Dinesh will prepare the accuracy report by 12 September.",
    "No final decision was made.",
  ],

  output: [
    "One-line summary: The team discussed changing the weekly stock-count day, but approval is pending additional accuracy data.",
    "Key point: Operations prefers Wednesday because Thursday outbound activity has increased.",
    "Decision status: No final decision was made.",
    "Action item: Dinesh to prepare two weeks of stock-count accuracy data by 12 September.",
    "Open item: Finance will need the requested data before the proposed schedule change can be approved.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Shorter is not always better",
    description:
      "A summary that removes a deadline, exception, control or unresolved decision may be shorter but materially less accurate.",
  },
  {
    title: "Watch proposal vs decision",
    description:
      "Words such as discussed, proposed and recommended should not become agreed, approved or confirmed in the summary.",
  },
  {
    title: "Protect numbers and conditions",
    description:
      "Dates, quantities, percentages, responsibilities and phrases such as 'only if' or 'subject to' can completely change the meaning when omitted.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Text Summarizer Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Summarize reports, emails, articles, meetings and documents while preserving important facts, numbers, decisions, actions, conditions and uncertainty."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="WRITING"
    title="Text"
    accentTitle="Summarizer."
    description="Turn long text into a clear, useful summary without losing important facts, numbers, decisions, actions, conditions or uncertainty."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Summarization",
      "Document analysis",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Reports",
      "Emails",
      "Meeting notes",
      "Articles",
      "Research",
      "Work documents",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Summarize the source without changing what it actually says."
    introDescription="Remove repetition and low-value detail while preserving the facts, decisions, responsibilities, deadlines and conditions that matter."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Keep a proposed change separate from an approved decision."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Make summaries shorter without making them less accurate."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Need to improve the original document?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Work Document Simplifier when the goal is to rewrite the document itself in clearer language rather than produce a separate summary."
    warningTitle="A concise summary can still be misleading"
    warningText="Always check important numbers, dates, decisions, action owners, deadlines and conditional wording against the original source. Never allow missing information to be filled with invented context."
  />
</BaseLayout>